WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 1.5.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:04.580
Stablecoins and how they break.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.645
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:06.645 --> 00:00:09.101
able to classify a stablecoin by design and

00:00:09.101 --> 00:00:10.775
name what could break its peg.

00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:13.365
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.365 --> 00:00:15.050
First, the key ideas.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.765
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:19.290
Then, your checklist.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.452
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:22.752 --> 00:00:26.823
Peg mechanics: if a stablecoin trades at $0.98

00:00:26.823 --> 00:00:30.981
and can be redeemed for $1.00, arbitrageurs buy

00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:34.832
and redeem it, pushing the price back up.

00:00:34.832 --> 00:00:36.475
The peg is only as strong

00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:39.367
as redemption access and reserve quality.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:44.246
History (research these as case studies): Fiat-backed, Cash/treasuries

00:00:44.246 --> 00:00:47.407
held by an issuer, Issuer, bank and custodian

00:00:47.407 --> 00:00:51.418
risk; freezes; redemption limited to approved parties.

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:58.038
Crypto-backed, Over-collateralised on-chain crypto, Collateral crash, liquidations, oracle failure.

00:00:58.038 --> 00:01:02.792
Synthetic / hedged, Derivative positions (e.g. spot + short

00:01:02.792 --> 00:01:08.083
perp), Funding turns negative, exchange/venue risk, model risk.

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:11.342
Algorithmic, Mostly its own mechanism / sister

00:01:11.342 --> 00:01:15.016
token, Reflexive death spiral when confidence breaks.

00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:18.832
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:18.832 --> 00:01:22.547
A fiat-backed coin trades at $0.97.

00:01:22.547 --> 00:01:26.440
Redemption is $1.00 minus a 0.1% fee,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:31.247
but only for verified institutional minters.

00:01:31.247 --> 00:01:34.341
A minter makes about 2.9% per coin by buying

00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:38.147
and redeeming, so arbitrage should restore the peg.

00:01:38.147 --> 00:01:40.521
You can't redeem, so you rely on minters

00:01:40.521 --> 00:01:43.022
acting and on the reserves being real.

00:01:43.022 --> 00:01:45.670
If markets doubt the reserves, minters may not

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.642
step in, and the discount can grow.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.402
Here's your checklist.

00:01:50.402 --> 00:01:51.937
Do it now, for real.

00:01:51.937 --> 00:01:53.503
First, I know the type and

00:01:53.503 --> 00:01:55.832
backing of every stablecoin I hold.

00:01:55.832 --> 00:01:58.157
Next, I know who can redeem, and how.

00:01:58.157 --> 00:02:01.160
Then, I don't treat "different stablecoins" as

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.527
diversified if they share issuers or collateral.

00:02:04.527 --> 00:02:07.473
And finally, I have a depeg exit

00:02:07.473 --> 00:02:10.866
rule (e.g. sell or rotate below $0.99

00:02:10.866 --> 00:02:13.098
for longer than X hours).

00:02:14.398 --> 00:02:15.608
Question 1.

00:02:15.608 --> 00:02:18.273
What keeps a fiat-backed stablecoin near $1?

00:02:22.693 --> 00:02:24.981
The answer: Redemption for $1 of

00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:27.963
reserves, plus arbitrageurs who can redeem.

00:02:29.263 --> 00:02:30.508
Question 2.

00:02:30.508 --> 00:02:32.583
Why are algorithmic designs fragile?

00:02:37.003 --> 00:02:38.458
The answer: They rely on

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:40.563
confidence and their own token.

00:02:40.563 --> 00:02:43.983
When demand falls, the mechanism can amplify the fall.

00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:46.553
Question 3.

00:02:46.553 --> 00:02:49.823
Two stablecoins both backed by the same collateral.

00:02:49.823 --> 00:02:50.768
Diversified?

00:02:55.188 --> 00:02:58.173
The answer: No. They share the same failure point.

00:02:59.473 --> 00:03:01.988
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:01.988 --> 00:03:05.043
Next up, Lesson 1.6: Scam defence.
